.\" Manual page for wifi-hotspot
.TH WIFI\-HOTSPOT 8 "2026\-10\-19" "wifi\-hotspot" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
wifi\-hotspot \- share a Wi\-Fi client uplink as an access point
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwifi\-hotspot\fR { up | down | restart | sync | status | detect }
\fBwifi\-hotspot up\fR [ \fB\-\-mode\fR \fIauto\fR | \fIsame\fR | \fIp2p\fR ]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wifi\-hotspot
runs a Wi\-Fi access point (hotspot) while the primary interface
.RB ( wlan0 )
stays associated to a Wi\-Fi network as a client, and forwards the hotspot
clients' traffic to that uplink with NAT.
.PP
It exists because of a hardware limitation of single\-radio adapters: a client
and an access point can coexist only on one channel.  Such adapters advertise
this through
.BR iw (8);
for example, the Realtek RTL8852BE
.RB ( rtw89 )
reports:
.PP
.nf
    * #{ managed } <= 1, #{ AP, P2P\-client, P2P\-GO } <= 1, total <= 2, #channels <= 1
    * #{ managed } <= 1, #{ P2P\-client, P2P\-GO } <= 1,       total <= 2, #channels <= 2
.fi
.PP
The first combination allows a client and an AP, but only on the
.I same channel
(same\-channel concurrency).  NetworkManager cannot drive both roles itself:
activating a hotspot tears down the client connection, and its
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
refuses to grab a second interface.  Windows instead silently puts the hotspot
on the client's channel, and
.B wifi\-hotspot
does the same thing on Linux.
.PP
The command therefore has two ways to run the hotspot:
.TP
.B same
Create the
.B ap0
interface, assign it a unique locally administered MAC address, run
.BR hostapd (8)
pinned to the uplink's channel, and start
.BR dnsmasq (8)
for DHCP and DNS.  This is used whenever the uplink's channel can host an
access point, and it uses the radio at full speed.
.TP
.B p2p
When the uplink's channel cannot host an access point \- a radar/DFS channel, a
channel marked
.I no IR
for initiating radiation, or 6\ GHz \- use the second combination above: ask the
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
that NetworkManager already runs for a Wi\-Fi Direct group owner, which may use a
channel of its own.  The group appears as a
.I p2p\-wlan0\-N
interface, which then gets the same addressing, DHCP and NAT treatment.  The
radio hops between the two channels, so this is slower and
.I experimental
(see
.BR NOTES ).
.PP
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
can drive Wi\-Fi Direct in two layouts and either one works.  On drivers that
expose a
.I P2P\-device
interface (Intel's
.IR iwlwifi )
it runs P2P on a dedicated device interface, and the control socket is
.IR /run/wpa_supplicant/p2p\-dev\-CLIENT .
On drivers that do not (Realtek's
.IR rtw89 ,
used by the RTL8852BE) it never creates that interface and drives P2P on the
client interface itself, with the control socket
.IR /run/wpa_supplicant/CLIENT .
A missing
.I P2P\-device
entry therefore does not mean Wi\-Fi Direct is unavailable.  What the driver must
permit is a group owner next to a managed interface on a channel of its own
\- the second combination above.  The
.B detect
command asks the running
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
which layout applies.
.PP
Which of the two is used is controlled by the
.B MODE
setting (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
In both cases the required NAT, forwarding and firewall rules are added and
removed again on
.BR down .
.PP
The
.B detect
command reports what the card, the current connection and the system support,
and which of the two ways will be used.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B up
Create or ensure the hotspot.  With
.BR MODE=auto
(default) it uses
.B same
if the client's current channel can host an access point, otherwise
.BR p2p ;
the decision is logged.  Idempotent: if the hotspot is already up in the
required mode this only refreshes the network and firewall rules.
.TP
.B down
Tear the hotspot down: stop hostapd and dnsmasq, remove the Wi\-Fi Direct group
(if one is up), delete the firewall and NAT rules and delete the
.B ap0
interface.  The client connection is left untouched.
.TP
.B restart
Equivalent to
.B down
followed by
.BR up .
Use after changing the mode, SSID or password.
.TP
.B sync
Re\-pin the hotspot to the client's current channel.  In
.B same
mode it does nothing if the client is disconnected or the channel already
matches; in
.B p2p
mode there is nothing to follow, so it only restarts the group if it has gone
away.  This is what the NetworkManager dispatcher calls on reconnect and
roaming.
.TP
.B status
Show the client SSID and frequency, the mode and AP interface in use, and
whether hostapd, dnsmasq and the NAT rule are up.  This is the default when no
command is given and it does
.I not
require root.
.TP
.B detect
Report what the machine can do, without changing anything and without root:
the interface, driver and current connection; whether a client and an access
point can coexist and on how many channels; whether a Wi\-Fi Direct group owner
may use its own channel; which channels a network may be started on; and the
mode that
.B up
will pick.  For Wi\-Fi Direct it also probes the running
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
to see whether P2P is running and on which control interface it lives, since
without that no group can be started.  That socket is only readable by root, so as
an ordinary user this one line is a guess and is reported as such.
Use it to tell a bug report or a
.B MODE
choice apart from a hardware limit.
.PP
Except for
.BR status
and
.BR detect ,
the commands re\-execute themselves as root using
.BR pkexec (1)
(falling back to
.BR sudo (8)),
so they may be run by an ordinary user.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-mode " " \fIauto\fR | \fIsame\fR | \fIp2p\fR
Available on
.BR up ,
.B restart
and
.BR sync .
Override the configured
.B MODE
for this invocation; takes precedence over the configuration file.  Accepts
either
.B \-\-mode \fIvalue\fR
or
.BR \-\-mode= \fIvalue\fR .
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/wifi\-hotspot.conf
is a shell fragment sourced by the script.  All settings are optional and
default to the values shown.  Uncomment and edit as needed.
.TP
.B CLIENT="wlan0"
The Wi\-Fi client interface whose connection is shared.
.TP
.B AP="ap0"
The virtual access point interface created and removed by the script in
.B same
mode.
.TP
.B SSID="ArchHotspot"
The hotspot SSID.  In
.B p2p
mode this is the name of the Wi\-Fi Direct group as well.
.TP
.B COUNTRY="KE"
Two\-letter ISO 3166 country code written as
.B country_code
to the hostapd configuration.  Set it so the regulatory domain permits
your AP channel.
.TP
.B AP_ADDR="10.42.0.1"
IPv4 address assigned to the hotspot interface, in either mode.
.TP
.B AP_PREFIX="24"
Prefix length of the hotspot subnet.
.TP
.B DHCP_RANGE="10.42.0.10,10.42.0.200,12h"
dnsmasq DHCP range (first address, last address, lease time).
.TP
.B RUN_DIR="/run"
Directory for pid, lease and state files.
.TP
.B MANAGE_FIREWALL=1
When 0, the script makes no firewall, forwarding or NAT changes at all.
Set this if you manage the firewall yourself (see
.BR FIREWALL ).
.TP
.B MODE="auto"
Which way to run the hotspot:
.RS
.TP
.B auto
Use
.B same
when the client's channel can host an access point, otherwise
.B p2p
if the card and wpa_supplicant support it.  The default.
.TP
.B same
Always use hostapd, pinned to the client's channel.
.TP
.B p2p
Always use a Wi\-Fi Direct group owner.  Requires a driver that permits a group
owner next to a client connection (see
.BR NOTES );
.B up
fails with an explanation if P2P is not available.
.RE
.TP
.B P2P_BAND="bg"
Band for the Wi\-Fi Direct group's own channel when
.B MODE
resolves to
.BR p2p :
.B bg
(2.4\ GHz, default channel 6) or
.B a
(5\ GHz, default channel 36).
.TP
.B P2P_CHANNEL="auto"
Channel number for the group, or
.B auto
for the band's default.  It must be a channel the card may start a network on;
otherwise the default is used with a warning.
.TP
.B WPA_CTRL_DIR="/run/wpa_supplicant"
Directory holding
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)'s
control sockets, used only in
.B p2p
mode.  The group is started on the socket that drives P2P: \c
.IR p2p\-dev\-CLIENT
when the driver gave
.B wpa_supplicant
a dedicated P2P device, and \c
.I CLIENT
itself when it did not.
.PP
The Wi\-Fi password is read once from the NetworkManager profile named
after
.B SSID
(or
.BR Hotspot ),
generated if not found, and stored in
.IR /etc/hostapd/ap0.psk .
To change it, delete that file and run
.B wifi\-hotspot restart
(and reconnect the clients).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WIFI_HOTSPOT_ELEVATE
Command used to re\-execute the script as root, e.g.
.BR sudo .
When unset,
.B pkexec
is used if available, otherwise
.BR sudo .
.SH FIREWALL
On a host whose firewall has a default\-drop policy for incoming and
forwarded traffic \- for example Arch's
.I /etc/nftables.conf
\- a plain
.BR iptables (8)
rule is
.I not
enough to make the hotspot work: a packet must be accepted by
.I every
base chain attached to a netfilter hook, so a
.B policy drop
in a native nftables chain silently overrides an
.B iptables
ACCEPT in a different chain.
.PP
When
.B MANAGE_FIREWALL
is 1,
.B wifi\-hotspot
additionally inserts a scoped
.B accept
rule for the hotspot interface (whichever mode is in use) into the existing
nftables
.I ip filter
.B INPUT
and
.B forward
chains.  The rules carry the comment
.RB ` wifi\-hotspot '
and are deleted again on
.BR down .
No nftables rules are added if the
.I ip filter
table does not exist.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/wifi\-hotspot
The script.
.TP
.I /etc/wifi\-hotspot.conf
Configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/hostapd/ap0.conf
Generated hostapd configuration (mode 0600), rewritten on every
.BR up
in
.B same
mode.
.TP
.I /etc/hostapd/ap0.psk
The hotspot passphrase (mode 0600).
.TP
.I /run/hostapd\-ap0.pid
hostapd pid file.
.TP
.I /run/dnsmasq\-ap0.pid
dnsmasq pid file.
.TP
.I /run/dnsmasq\-ap0.leases
dnsmasq DHCP leases.
.TP
.I /run/wifi\-hotspot\-ap0.state
Records the running mode and interface so that
.BR down ,
.B sync
and
.B status
know what to tear down; removed by
.BR down .
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/wifi\-hotspot.service
Oneshot unit that brings the hotspot up at boot.
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/90\-wifi\-hotspot
Dispatcher hook that runs
.B sync
on connect or roaming and
.B down
on disconnect.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B Same channel is mandatory in same mode
The hostapd AP is always pinned to the client's channel; this is a limit of the
radio, not of the tool.  The dispatcher does the pinning automatically
when you roam.
.TP
.B Wi\-Fi Direct mode is experimental
In
.B p2p
mode the group is started through the
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
control socket by
.BR wpa_cli (8).
It requires a card/driver that permits a group owner next to a client connection on
its own channel (reported by
.BR detect ),
and a
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
that has P2P running.  Whether P2P lives on a dedicated
.I P2P\-device
interface or on the client interface itself does not matter; see
.BR DESCRIPTION .
Cards that do not permit a group owner on its own channel keep
.B MODE=auto
on the same channel, as Windows does.  It also still uses one radio,
hopping between two channels, so throughput is lower than in
.B same
mode.  If it does not work,
.B MODE=same
is unaffected.  Note also that the passphrase is passed to
.B wpa_cli
as an argument, so it is briefly visible in the process list.
.TP
.B Throughput is roughly halved
One radio time\-shares between the two roles, on both links.  Share an
ethernet uplink instead for full speed.
.TP
.B 5 GHz DFS channels
Channels 52\-64 and 100\-144 require radar detection
.RB ( 802.11h )
and add a channel availability check (CAC) delay; non\-DFS 5 GHz and
2.4 GHz channels start immediately.  Because the delay is avoidable, such a
channel is one of the cases where
.B MODE=auto
prefers
.BR p2p .
.TP
.B Regulatory domain
The AP can only use channels allowed for
.B COUNTRY
in the current regulatory domain.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure \- for example hostapd could not start, the Wi\-Fi Direct group could
not be created, the client is not connected, or a required program is missing.
Diagnostic messages are printed to standard error and, when running under
systemd,
.B wifi\-hotspot.service
is logged to the journal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask what the machine can do:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ wifi\-hotspot detect           # as root, so the P2P line is definite

  wifi\-hotspot detect

  interface     : wlan0 (phy0)  [rtw89_8852be]
  client link   : connected to "MyNet" on channel 36 (5180 MHz)
  AP + client   : yes \- same channel only
  Wi\-Fi Direct  : yes \- group owner may use its own channel
  host channels : 2.4 GHz 1\-13, 5 GHz 36\-48
  this channel  : 36 (5180 MHz) \- can host an AP

  hostapd       : /usr/bin/hostapd
  dnsmasq       : /usr/bin/dnsmasq
  wpa_supplicant: /run/wpa_supplicant/wlan0 (P2P on the client interface)

  verdict       : ready \- 'wifi\-hotspot up' shares this connection on 36 (5180 MHz)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Bring the hotspot up and check it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# wifi\-hotspot up
$ wifi\-hotspot status
client wlan0 :  SSID: MyNet  freq: 5180.0
mode         : same (ap0)
ap     ap0   :  type AP  channel 36 (5180 MHz)
hostapd      : running
dnsmasq      : running
nat          : ? (need root)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Force the experimental mode, then tear the hotspot down:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# wifi\-hotspot up \-\-mode p2p
# wifi\-hotspot down
.RE
.fi
